   $q$Two random samples were taken from a town of 6,000 people to ask if they ride the town bus. Sample A: 30 riders out of 100 people. Sample B: 33 riders out of 150 people. Using both samples together, what is the best estimate of the number of bus riders in town?$q$, $q$[{"text":"1,320","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-only-one-sample"},{"text":"1,512","is_correct":true},{"text":"1,560","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"averaged-rates-of-unequal-samples"},{"text":"1,800","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-only-one-sample"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$1,512$q$, $q$Together there are $30 + 33 = 63$ riders out of $100 + 150 = 250$ people, a rate of $0.252$, and $0.252 \times 6000 = 1512$. The choice 1,560 averages the two rates, $0.30$ and $0.22$, which ignores that Sample B is larger.$q$, 'draft', null)
